  Today was a combination of final presentation preparation and team-building activities. During the first part of the day, my team and I continued practicing for the Final Research Symposium. We refined our presentation by reviewing each speaker's section, improving the flow between slides, and making sure we could clearly communicate our project's motivation, methodology, results, and contributions. Practicing multiple times helped us identify areas where we could explain technical concepts more naturally and improve the overall delivery of our presentation.

  After our presentation practice, we participated in a field day with the rest of the research cohort. We took part in several fun activities, including Capture the Flag, a blind cone race, soccer, and other team-based games. It was a refreshing break from the technical work we have been doing over the past several weeks and provided an opportunity to interact with other participants outside of the research environment.

  Overall, today's activities reminded me that research programs are not only about developing technical skills but also about building relationships, working effectively as a team, and enjoying the experience alongside fellow researchers. It was a great balance between preparing for an important presentation and spending time with the cohort before the program comes to an end.

